INT. MRI SUITE, UNIVERSITY HOSPITAL - NIGHT

A cold, white room dominated by the great ring of the scanner. A narrow table slides in and out of its throat. Behind a window, a console glows. A low mechanical thrum fills the air, like the breath of something asleep.

DR. AUGUSTINE LEHMANN, 50s, white coat, wire glasses, sits at the console behind the window. His fingers rest on the keys. He watches the monitors with the stillness of a man used to bad news.

NIAMH BRENNAN, 30s, lies on the scanner table in a paper gown, a blanket across her knees, a headset over her ears. Her eyes are closed. Her hands are folded on her stomach.

He presses a button. The table slides her slowly into the ring.

The thrum becomes a rhythmic knocking. Louder. Louder.

He leans to the intercom.

DR. LEHMANN
Niamh, can you hear me?

NIAMH
(muffled)
I hear a jackhammer.

DR. LEHMANN
That's normal. Keep still.

NIAMH
Is it supposed to hurt?

DR. LEHMANN
No.

NIAMH
Then why does it feel like it's looking back at me?

He studies the monitor. A grey slice of brain scrolls across the screen. He freezes it. Magnifies a small, dark shape. Frowns.

He does not tell her.

DR. LEHMANN
We're nearly done.

NIAMH
You said that ten minutes ago.

DR. LEHMANN
I meant it then too.

She laughs, one short breath. The scanner knocks. He types something. Deletes it. Types again.

She lifts one hand slightly. Opens her fingers.

NIAMH
Doctor?

DR. LEHMANN
Yes?

NIAMH
When you look at the pictures, what do you see?

DR. LEHMANN
(carefully)
I see a very interesting brain.

NIAMH
Is it interesting good, or interesting bad?

He doesn't answer. His hand hovers over the intercom. The table slides her out of the ring, slowly, until she is lying in the open white light.

He rises. Steps through the door. Crosses to her side. She opens her eyes and stares up at him.

NIAMH (CONT'D)
(softly)
There's something, isn't there.

DR. LEHMANN
(at the same moment)
There's something we need to discuss.

^NIAMH
There's something, isn't there.

Neither speaks. The scanner sighs and falls silent. She takes off the headset. He offers his hand. She takes it, and sits up with great care, the blanket sliding to the floor.

NIAMH (CONT'D)
Tell me here. Not in an office.

He nods. Pulls up a stool. Sits beside her, level with her eyes.

DR. LEHMANN
I'd like to run one more sequence.

NIAMH
And then?

DR. LEHMANN
And then I will tell you everything I know.

She nods. Lies back down. He steps behind the window again. The ring starts to knock.
